module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_citation_attests_a_decision_rule_the_code_does_not_implement" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "A module cites a STANDARD and models a decision procedure, but the decision the code implements is a weaker or different rule than the standard's normative one. The citation then attests properties the code does not have -- total order, precedence, canonicality -- and every reader who trusts the anchor inherits them. The anchor is current, the name resolves, and the sentence the code speaks is not the sentence the standard speaks.",

    "**THIS IS NOT THE TOOL-CLI/STANDARD MISMATCH, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE CLASS.** The neighbour class (fixed in gunbc#13189's review) is about WHO owns the surface: a module models a tool's CLI while citing the standard the tool implements. Here the owner is right -- the module really does model what the cited standard defines -- and what is wrong is the STRENGTH: the code path under the declaration implements a weaker rule than the standard's normative algorithm. That is worse on two counts. The pairing survives the placement gate, which correctly reads subject-to-authority ownership; and the citation is plausible on its face, so review has nothing visible to flag until someone reads the code path against the standard's text.",

    "**RECEIPT (vivid-ibex-684, 2026-10-04, gunbc#13189).** `dag/extdeps/version/semver.dag` scopes `semver_scheme` with a first citation to semver.org (Semantic Versioning 2.0.0), whose normative content for comparison is the PRECEDENCE algorithm in its section 11: identifiers composed only of digits compare numerically; identifiers containing letters or hyphens compare lexically in ASCII sort order; numeric identifiers ALWAYS have lower precedence than non-numeric ones (so `1.0.0-alpha` ranks below `1.0.0`); and a larger set of pre-release fields has higher precedence when all preceding identifiers are equal. The comparison path under the module calls `semver_identity_compare` -- a lexical string comparison that implements none of those rules and ranks `1.10.0` below `1.9.0`, the reverse of the standard's numeric order. Found by external side-chat review as REQUESTED CHANGES (`review 5404350654`), not by any mechanism in the tree; the module and its regenerated mirror were pulled back to the frontier byte-identical rather than fixed, so the defect remains live in the tree as of this row.",

    "**RECEIPT (vivid-ibex-684, 2026-10-04, gunbc#13258, side-chat review `5406002589`; defect live in the tree).** `dag/extdeps/accessibility/wcag_contrast.dag` cites WCAG 2.2 (www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#dfn-relative-luminance) and its comment asserts the linearization knee is the page's published constant at 0.03928 -- and the code does use 392/10000. But the cited definition specifies the threshold 0.04045 and notes 0.03928 as the older value, so the citation attests a definition the code does not implement. The module was authored 2026-07-21 with the comment noting the page as retrieved that day, so the pairing was already false when authored -- there is no upstream-drift story here, only a misattestation that name-level checks (live URL, subject ownership) do not see. Pulled from #13258 per that review rather than fixed; the defect is live as of this receipt.",

    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-05, frontier; semver half).** `dag/extdeps/version/semver.dag`'s `semver_identity_compare` now parses the label back into the typed form over the SemVer 2.0.0 section 2 grammar and routes through `semver_compare`, the module's one implementation of the section 11 precedence rule; a label outside the grammar is refused by the scheme's compare through the `VersionComparison` carrier (`extdeps.version`: `VersionCompared { ordering }` | `VersionComparisonRefused { side, identity, cause }`) -- a located refusal naming the failing side, the label, and the cause, pinned positively by `w_semver_scheme_out_of_grammar_label_refuses_precedence` -- with the sibling git and go scheme instances updated to the same carrier. The cited source was re-read live first (semver.org/#spec-item-11, retrieved 2026-10-05). Five discriminating reds failed for the documented reason before the fix (`1.10.0` vs `1.9.0` ranked Less; release above pre-release reversed; numeric-vs-alphanumeric and numeric-width pre-release cases ranked by ASCII; build metadata ranked) and pass after (dag/test/claim/extdeps_version_semver_witness_test.dag, remote claim_batch --claim-run). The regenerated stage0 mirrors are installed and `claim_executor --required-regen` now adjudicates the whole tree clean (declared_divergent=1 [main.rs] only). The wcag half of this row is repaired in a separate PR (#13414).",

    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-05, review round on the same PR; semver half hardened, sibling schemes de-fabricated).** Follow-up review found the first repair incomplete in three ways, each fixed at its owner. (1) `semver_identifier_parse` accepted a pre-release identifier that is all digits with a leading zero (`1.0.0-01`): section 9 forbids leading zeroes in numeric identifiers and the section 2 grammar requires an alphanumeric identifier to contain a non-digit, so such a segment is out of grammar -- it now refuses (strict mode), pinned by `w_semver_pre_release_leading_zero_numeric_label_refuses`, which failed for the documented reason on the pre-fix head (the segment ranked as alphanumeric) and passes after. (2) The pre-release and build identifier parses were byte-identical and a `strict_numeric` flag did nothing; there is now ONE identifier parse carrying the only real difference between the section 9 and section 10 grammars -- strict mode refuses the leading-zero digit run, permissive mode (build, where section 10 permits leading zeroes, `1.0.0+0130`) keeps it alphanumeric -- pinned by `w_semver_build_leading_zero_identifier_still_parses`. (2b) A follow-up side-chat review found the bare `Ordering?` refusal not located and the go scheme's own comparator still widening (`v1.0.0-alpha` ranked Equal to `v1.0.0-beta`, its parser accepting `v1.0.0-01` and empty identifier components): the compare now returns the closed `VersionComparison` carrier (`VersionCompared { ordering }` | `VersionComparisonRefused { side: ComparisonSide, identity, cause }` — the side is the closed coproduct `LeftOperand | RightOperand`, so a misspelled side is unwritable) across all three schemes, the refusal's cause states the grammar honestly rather than claiming a specific defect the failing label may not have, and go deletes its second partial implementation entirely (prefix-strip + SemVer grammar + `semver_compare`), with new reds `w_go_module_version_scheme_orders_pre_release_identifiers` and `w_go_module_version_scheme_refuses_leading_zero_pre_release` and `w_go_module_version_scheme_refuses_label_without_v_prefix`. (3) The sibling git and go schemes still fabricated answers: git returned Equal/Greater by string equality and go returned Less for any unequal pair, each wrapped as Present; the only git-scheme consumer was an equality-only witness and the go scheme had no consumers, so no caller relied on the wrong behaviour. Git now consumes extdeps.version.semver's core parse (a git release version IS the SemVer core triple -- one grammar, no re-invention) and routes through `git_release_version_compare`; go strips its `v` prefix and reuses the SemVer grammar and `semver_compare` (the deleted go-local comparator ranked ANY two pre-release versions Equal -- `v1.0.0-alpha == v1.0.0-beta` -- the exact widening the cited rule forbids), both refusing through the `VersionComparisonRefused` carrier a label outside their grammars -- pinned by `w_git_release_version_scheme_orders_minor_numerically`, `w_git_release_version_scheme_refuses_out_of_grammar_label`, `w_go_module_version_scheme_orders_patch_numerically`, `w_go_module_version_scheme_refuses_unparsable_label`, each of which failed for the documented reason on the pre-fix head (placeholder answers wrapped as Present) and passes after (remote claim_batch --claim-run, 2026-10-05). `claim_executor --required-regen` re-adjudicates the whole tree clean after the changes (first_generation_equal=true, declared_divergent=1 [main.rs] only).",

    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-06, second review round; parser still minted orders for two out-of-grammar shapes).** Follow-up review (review 76945) found two holes in the new section 2 parse, each fixed at its owner in `dag/extdeps/version/semver.dag`, with the hole on the git scheme closing automatically since git consumes this parse. (1) `1.2.3.` parsed as `1.2.3`: the last field was reached through a dot-split, so the trailing delimiter was dropped and an out-of-grammar label ranked. The last field now consumes the WHOLE remainder (a leftover delimiter then fails the numeric run), pinned by `w_semver_core_refuses_trailing_delimiter_label`, which mints `Greater` under the old code and refuses after. (2) `semver_digits_to_int` accumulated `acc * 10 + d` with unchecked `Int` arithmetic before the `checked_int_to_nat` conversion, so a 20-nines field wrapped into a small positive Int that passed the Nat check and minted a wrong precedence; the arithmetic was first re-done through `std.checked_arithmetic`, and then - see the next receipt - the conversion left the comparison path entirely. Both reds are remote claim_batch green on the fixed head (2026-10-06). A third fix in the same round is prose-only: the row's own receipt string quoted the stripped prefix with raw double quotes, which closed the .dag string literal and left a bare `v` parsed as an unimported identifier (the floor's UnimportedBareProvider refusal); backticks instead.",

    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-06, third review round; the overflow refusal itself was a grammar invention).** The side chat (review 5427723272) rejected the previous round's overflow half: section 2 and section 9 numerics have NO size limit, so `1.99999999999999999999.0` is a valid label that must compare above `1.2.0`, and refusing it as out-of-grammar was inventing a failure the spec does not describe. The comparison path now carries numeric components as arbitrary-width digit sequences (code points, admitted with no leading zero except the single digit 0, per sections 2 and 9; build identifiers stay validated text with no conversion anywhere), and precedence compares by digit-run length and then ASCII digit order - section 11's numeric comparison at any width. `semver_digits_to_int` and every `checked_int` use are deleted. Controls: `w_semver_core_wide_numeric_field_compares_numerically` (20 nines in the minor field ranks Greater), `w_pre_release_wide_numeric_identifier_compares_numerically` (wide numeric pre-release above 1.0.0-2), `w_build_wide_numeric_identifier_is_valid_and_ignored` (wide build metadata parses and compares Equal).",
    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-06, fourth and fifth review rounds; the arbitrary-width field was forgeable and three live consumers still held raw records).** Side-chat review 5429823519 rejected the type-only confinement: `SemVerNumericField` was a bare alias of `List<Int>`, so nothing in the language enforced non-empty, ASCII-digits-only, or no-leading-zero, external code could forge `[65]` or `[48,49]`, and compare still ordered the forgeries. The field is now a one-field record whose only producer is the validating constructor `semver_numeric_field` (refuses empty, non-ASCII-digit, and leading-zero sequences; admits the single digit 0), pinned by `w_numeric_field_constructor_refuses_forged_sequences`; the git anchor and every witness value derive through that constructor or the parser, never raw code-point lists. The honest rung, stated in both the type comment and the witness file: the emitter lowers the one-field record to a plain alias, so the confinement is BY-SOURCE (only the constructor produces field values; every consumer derives through it or the parser) plus the executing negative controls -- not a runtime type guarantee. Review 77150 then found the ripple the type change owed outside its own files: `SemVerVersion` field literals with raw Int values still stood in `dag/extdeps/tailscale/serve.dag`, `dag/gunbc/live_deploy/spec.dag`, and `dag/test/claim/live_deploy/emit_test.dag` (enumerated by name and migrated), each would have projected `.digits` off an Int at execution. All three now derive their version values through `semver_version_parse`, and the serve-off boundary plus the observed client version are the parse RESULT (Optionals) -- so an unadmitted label is carried to the consumer as a refusal, not a fabricated version; the same repair removed the git anchor's unreachable-but-fabricating `0.0.0` fallback (the anchor is the parse result, consumers unwrap, `w_git_2_0_anchor` pins the admitted label), rewrote every witness SemVerVersion/identifier literal through the parser or the confined constructor, and deleted the stale overflow-refusal comment the width round had superseded. Local parse-check of every touched entry: 0 blocking errors.",
    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-07, sixth review round; the confinement was by-source only and the comparator was a raw-list bypass).** Side-chat review 5435818773 accepted the consumer migration but blocked twice. (1) The type-level seal was missing: `type SemVerNumericField { digits: List<Int> }` had a public constructor, so any module could still mint `SemVerNumericField { digits: [48,49] }` and the negative control only proved the checking fn returns Absent. The field is now `sole_constructor` -- constructible only inside extdeps.version.semver -- and the witness file executes an OUTSIDE-MODULE compile probe over the three forged literals (leading-zero run, non-digit code point, empty run), each required to meet the SoleConstructorViolation refusal, plus the via-constructor positive control (compile_dag_diagnostic_census + census_rows_of_class); the native route's modifier-parse gap remains cited, not restated (g0_type_decl_modifier_parse_without_sealing_property). (2) `semver_compare_numeric_digits(a: List<Int>, b: List<Int>)` was itself a raw-list bypass that git imported; it now takes two confined SemVerNumericField values and projects `.digits` internally, with the run-length/lexical helper internal over plain lists -- no raw code-point list reaches the comparator. Mirror regenerated (required-regen first_generation_equal=true, 163/163, declared_divergent=1 [main.rs] unchanged); witness floors on the pushed head: semver 32 PASS exit 0 (both new probe witnesses green), git 10/10 exit 0, go 24/24 exit 0.",


    "**REPAIR RECEIPT (vivid-lynx-377, 2026-10-05, frontier; wcag half).** `dag/extdeps/accessibility/wcag_contrast.dag`'s linearization knee is now the cited 0.04045 (integer `c_scaled <= 404` at scale 10000) and the comment cites the definition's Note 2 recording 0.03928 as the threshold before May 2021 (live page re-read 2026-10-05). The threshold half of the #13258 receipt was checked against the live page and already matched: 450/300/700 transcribe SC 1.4.3 (4.5:1 normal, 3:1 large-scale), SC 1.4.11 (3:1 graphical objects) and SC 1.4.6 (7:1 AAA); pinned by `witness_cited_knee_threshold_rows_match_criterion_text`, no change made. The knee's discriminating red samples the only window where the two constants disagree -- no 8-bit channel at unity white lands there (10/255 = 0.03922 is below both knees), which is why the module's earlier pins never saw it -- and pins the outcome flip: `linearize_channel_scaled(393)` returns 30 under the cited knee (29 under the defective one), and the colour pair (393,0,500)/(8000,1000,8000) FAILS AA normal text under the cited knee while the defective knee called it sufficient. Both green after the fix (dag/test/claim/wcag_contrast_witness_test.dag, remote claim_batch --claim-run). The semver half of this row is repaired in a separate PR (#13412).",

    "**WHY NO EXISTING WALL SEES IT, stated as a boundary rather than a gap to be closed by the neighbour.** The extdeps scope placement gate adjudicates the diff-added pairing of a subject declaration to a citation anchor at the level of NAMES: it can see that a subject is not the cited upstream's surface (tool CLI vs standard), because that mismatch is visible in what the module declares and what the anchor names. The lexical-vs-precedence difference lives in the code the declaration dispatches through, not in any name, module path, or anchor field; DESIGN section 4c makes annotations invisible to semantic passes, and a scope block's citation is an annotation about authority, not a type on the code path. So a fully green placement gate and a green namespace wave are both positive evidence about ownership and binding, and NO evidence about whether the decision the code implements is the one the standard defines.",

    "**THE SAME FAILURE WITHOUT A CITATION HUNK.** The false attestation propagates: any caller that takes a module's compared/verified value as standard-ordered inherits the gap, and any new comparison path added under an already-cited module inherits the citation without touching prose. Neither edit produces a citation hunk the recognition rule below would see, which is why the rule names the code path, not only the anchor.",

    "**RUNG: MITIGATABLE.** External review caught the instance; nothing in the tree can. The ceiling is not structural impossibility: whether a code path implements a cited algorithm is decidable for transcribed algorithms by comparing against a transcription datum of the standard's rules, so the honest ceiling is MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE. **NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a cited-surface census** -- an instrument that takes each ExternalModelScope's citation plus subject, extracts the normative operation the cited upstream defines for the surface the subject names (precedence, canonical form, grammar production), and surfaces, for adjudication, the actual code path the declaration dispatches through -- refusing the pairing until an adjudicator dispositions whether the path is that operation or a weaker one. That is a real instrument and it does not exist; naming it here is the trigger, not a claim of coverage.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE: two triggers, and the second matters because the defect recurs with no citation hunk.** (1) Any diff that adds or re-points a citation onto a module whose declaration dispatches through a comparison, verification, or production path not named by the cited upstream's normative operation. (2) Any diff that adds or changes the comparison, verification, or production dispatch under a declaration whose module ALREADY carries a citation -- the citation hunk never appears in such a diff, so anchor-level review sees nothing, while the false attestation extends to the new path. A third channel needs no diff at all: upstream drift, where a cited page is revised under a static citation and the pairing silently becomes false. No repository specimen of that third channel has been confirmed yet (the wcag_contrast receipt looked like one and was not: the pairing was false when authored), so it is named here as a channel to watch, not a counted instance. The obligation is that a scope block which cites a standard has adopted the standard's ALGORITHM as an attestation: read the code path UNDER THE DISPATCHED PRODUCTION CALL, not only the anchor and not a helper beside the path, and either match it against the standard's normative operation or scope a weaker subject whose surface the path really implements. Reading the anchor alone is the half that manufactures the false attestation; reading a helper beside the production path is the other half, and both were done once each while authoring this row.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
